# Raw RESP loadgen streams are reproducible from seed and exceed GitHub's
# 100 MB limit — keep them on disk, never in git (reports + summaries are
# the committed evidence; a campaign script must add specific run dirs,
# never a bare `git add -f .artifacts/`).
.artifacts/**/*.resp

# Same rule, same reason, for the soaks' document-leg logs: `redis-cli
# --pipe` writes one summary line per iteration, so a 32 h run leaves
# 1.4 M lines / 686 MB (one file 568 MB, past GitHub's limit) carrying
# nothing the committed `loadgen-doc-summary.md` does not state exactly.
.artifacts/**/loadgen-doc_*.log

# Raw `perf` sample files are never committed — they are hundreds of MB to
# gigabytes of machine-local sample data, they are not readable evidence,
# and GitHub hard-rejects anything over 100 MB (one `jget-read.perf` came
# to 1030 MB and blocked a push, 2026-08-16). The *extracted* artifacts —
# `perf report` output, the banned-symbol grep, the verdict — are what a
# reviewer cites, and those ARE committed. The S24 phase-7 runbook already
# states the rule for campaign flamegraphs ("delete perf.data
# immediately"); these patterns make it structural instead of a habit.
.artifacts/**/*.perf
.artifacts/**/perf.data
.artifacts/**/perf.data.*
.artifacts/**/*.perf.script
